Subject: vulkan/gpu: AMD's integrated graphics import host memory again

libplacebo 7.387 marks AMD's proprietary driver as slow to import host
memory (host_ptr_slow), after FFmpeg's pull request 24816 measured that on
a discrete Radeon over PCIe. Frames from memory are then copied by the CPU
into a buffer of the driver's before the GPU takes them. On integrated
graphics, whose memory is the host's, that copy is what is slow: on a
Ryzen 5 5600G's Radeon Vega 7 under Windows it took 70 to 80 ms of a core
for each 4K frame, libplacebo tone mapped 4K at 12 frames a second instead
of 70, and a 4K HDR transcode made 0.34 times real time instead of 1.36.
The mark stays on discrete AMD graphics and NVIDIA's.

--- a/src/vulkan/gpu.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/gpu.c
@@ -542,8 +542,11 @@
         .max_buffer_texels  = PL_MIN(limits.maxTexelBufferElements, max_size),
         .align_host_ptr     = host_props.minImportedHostPointerAlignment,
         .host_cached        = vk_malloc_avail(vk->ma, VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT),
+        // Changed for Pley, 2026-11: not on AMD's integrated graphics, whose
+        // memory is the host's, where an import keeps the CPU from copying.
         .host_ptr_slow      = driver_props.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_NVIDIA_PROPRIETARY ||
-                              driver_props.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_AMD_PROPRIETARY,
+                              (driver_props.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_AMD_PROPRIETARY &&
+                               props.properties.deviceType != VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_INTEGRATED_GPU),
         // pl_tex
         .max_tex_1d_dim     = limits.maxImageDimension1D,
         .max_tex_2d_dim     = limits.maxImageDimension2D,
